From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_pr_fixes_use_quaran <>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 20:45:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/8] os/mm: Add a quarantine so freed chunks are not reusable
 immediately

The poison and verify detector only watches a chunk while it is on the
free list, and the check runs at the moment the chunk is handed out.
The allocator hands a just freed chunk straight back, because it is a
perfect fit for the next request of the same size, so the window in
which a stale write can be caught is often a single malloc call wide.

If one thread frees a block, a second thread allocates it, and the
first thread only then writes through its stale pointer, the write
falls outside that window. It lands in memory which legitimately
belongs to the second thread, so there is no pattern left to compare
against and no check of the contents could tell the two writers apart.
This is not a limit of the implementation: at the allocator level both
writes are a store to a validly allocated address.

Delaying the reuse is what separates them, and on this platform it is
the only option, since the alternatives need an MMU or hardware pointer
tagging.

On free, offer the chunk to a bounded ring instead of returning it to
the free list. A held chunk keeps MM_ALLOC_BIT set, which is what stops
malloc from finding it and stops the neighbours from merging into it,
so its address cannot reach anybody else. When it ages out, the pattern
is checked and the chunk is coalesced and released as usual.

The ring lives in struct mm_heap_s rather than in the chunks. Putting
the links in the payload, as the delay list does, would place them
exactly where the write being hunted usually lands, so the corruption
would destroy the list instead of being reported.

Because a held chunk still looks allocated, the existing double free
test in mm_free() cannot see it, and a second free would enter the same
chunk into the ring twice. Scan the ring explicitly. That also turns a
double free of a held chunk into a report which names both callers.

malloc and memalign flush the whole quarantine before reporting
failure, so holding memory back for debugging never turns into an
allocation failure that would not happen with the option off.

The coalescing tail of mm_free_internal() becomes mm_free_coalesce() so
that the release path can reuse it.
---
 os/Kconfig.debug              |  65 ++++++++++
 os/include/tinyara/mm/mm.h    |  22 ++++
 os/mm/mm_heap/Make.defs       |   4 +
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_free.c       |  61 ++++++++-
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_initialize.c |   4 +
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_malloc.c     |  26 ++++
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_memalign.c   |  26 ++++
 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_quarantine.c | 224 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 8 files changed, 428 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 os/mm/mm_heap/mm_quarantine.c

diff --git a/os/Kconfig.debug b/os/Kconfig.debug
index 79dc55d1..85d72bc1 100644
--- a/os/Kconfig.debug
+++ b/os/Kconfig.debug
@@ -828,6 +828,71 @@ config DEBUG_MM_UAF_PANIC
 		If disabled, the corruption is only reported and the allocation
 		proceeds.
 
+config DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	bool "Delay the reuse of freed chunks"
+	default n
+	---help---
+		Hold a freed chunk out of the free list for a while instead of making
+		it reusable straight away, and check its poison pattern when it is
+		finally released.
+
+		This is what makes the case DEBUG_MM_UAF cannot see detectable: one
+		thread frees a block, a second thread allocates the same block, and
+		the first thread then writes through its stale pointer. Without the
+		delay the address already belongs to the second thread by the time the
+		bad write happens, so no check of the contents can tell that write
+		apart from a legitimate one. While the chunk is held, the second
+		thread is given a different one, so the stale write lands in memory
+		nobody owns and the pattern check finds it.
+
+		A held chunk keeps its allocated bit set, so it does not merge with
+		its neighbours. Its owner, allocating address and freeing address
+		therefore survive intact and the report names the right code. Freeing
+		a held chunk a second time is also reported, with both callers.
+
+		malloc releases the whole quarantine before it reports failure, so
+		enabling this does not cause allocation failures which would not
+		happen otherwise.
+
+		This widens the window in which a stale write is detectable, it does
+		not close it. A write which happens after the chunk has aged out and
+		been reallocated is still missed, and reads through a stale pointer
+		are not detected at all. Holding chunks back also costs contiguous
+		free space, which matters most on a small heap, so this is intended
+		for test and soak builds rather than for a product configuration.
+
+if DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+
+config DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE_CHUNKS
+	int "Maximum number of chunks to hold"
+	default 32
+	range 1 256
+	---help---
+		Size of the quarantine ring. Costs one pointer of static memory per
+		slot per heap. A larger ring keeps addresses out of circulation for
+		longer, which widens the window in which a stale write is still
+		detectable.
+
+config DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE_BYTES
+	int "Maximum number of bytes to hold"
+	default 4096
+	range 64 65536
+	---help---
+		Total size of the chunks held at any one time. The oldest chunks are
+		released to stay within this budget. Keep it small relative to the
+		heap: the memory is unavailable until it is released.
+
+config DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE_MAX_SIZE
+	int "Largest chunk to hold (bytes)"
+	default 1024
+	range 32 65536
+	---help---
+		Chunks larger than this go straight back to the free list instead of
+		being held. Holding a large buffer costs more of a small heap than
+		the extra detection is worth.
+
+endif # DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+
 endif # DEBUG_MM_UAF
 
 config DEBUG_IRQ
diff --git a/os/include/tinyara/mm/mm.h b/os/include/tinyara/mm/mm.h
index 635efcbe..07300ea4 100644
--- a/os/include/tinyara/mm/mm.h
+++ b/os/include/tinyara/mm/mm.h
@@ -411,6 +411,19 @@ struct mm_heap_s {
 
 	FAR struct mm_delaynode_s *mm_delaylist[CONFIG_SMP_NCPUS];
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	/* Chunks which have been freed but are deliberately not reusable yet, so
+	 * that a write through a stale pointer lands in memory nobody owns and
+	 * can still be recognised. Held out of band, in this array rather than in
+	 * the chunks themselves, so that the very write being hunted cannot
+	 * corrupt the list.
+	 */
+
+	FAR struct mm_allocnode_s *mm_quarantine[CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE_CHUNKS];
+	uint16_t mm_qhead;			/* Slot of the oldest held chunk */
+	uint16_t mm_qcount;			/* Number of chunks held */
+	size_t mm_qbytes;			/* Total size of the chunks held */
+#endif
 };
 
 /****************************************************************************
@@ -664,6 +677,15 @@ void mm_shrinkchunk(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR struct mm_allocnode_s *node,
 
 void mm_addfreechunk(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR struct mm_freenode_s *node);
 
+void mm_free_coalesce(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR struct mm_freenode_s *node);
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+void mm_quarantine_init(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap);
+bool mm_quarantine_contains(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR struct mm_allocnode_s *node);
+bool mm_quarantine_add(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR struct mm_allocnode_s *node);
+size_t mm_quarantine_flush(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap);
+#endif
+
 /* Functions contained in mm_size2ndx.c.c ***********************************/
 
 int mm_size2ndx(size_t size);
diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/Make.defs b/os/mm/mm_heap/Make.defs
index 732c094c..5012ec65 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/Make.defs
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/Make.defs
@@ -66,6 +66,10 @@ ifeq ($(CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF),y)
 CSRCS += mm_uaf.c
 endif
 
+ifeq ($(CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE),y)
+CSRCS += mm_quarantine.c
+endif
+
 ifeq ($(CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_HEAPINFO),y)
 CSRCS += mm_heapinfo_parse_heap.c mm_heapinfo_utils.c
 ifeq ($(CONFIG_HEAPINFO_USER_GROUP),y)
diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_free.c b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_free.c
index 6b7043e1..079404ef 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_free.c
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_free.c
@@ -121,8 +121,6 @@ static void mm_add_delaylist(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR void *mem)
 static void mm_free_internal(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR void *mem, mmaddress_t free_call_addr, pid_t free_call_pid)
 {
 	FAR struct mm_freenode_s *node;
-	FAR struct mm_freenode_s *prev;
-	FAR struct mm_freenode_s *next;
 	char task_name[CONFIG_TASK_NAME_SIZE + 1];
 
 	mvdbg("Freeing %p\n", mem);
@@ -185,17 +183,73 @@ static void mm_free_internal(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR void *mem, mmaddres
 		mm_givesemaphore(heap);
 		return;
 	}
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	if (mm_quarantine_contains(heap, (FAR struct mm_allocnode_s *)node)) {
+		/* The chunk is being held in the quarantine, so it still looks
+		 * allocated and the test above could not catch this. Freeing it again
+		 * is a double free, and unlike the usual case both callers are known.
+		 */
+
+		mdbg("WARNING!! Double free of a pointer which is still in quarantine\n");
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_FREEINFO
+		GET_TASK_NAME_BY_PID(task_name, node->free_call_pid);
+		mdbg("1st free: released by %s (%d) at addr 0x%08x\n", task_name, node->free_call_pid, node->free_call_addr);
+#endif
+		GET_TASK_NAME_BY_PID(task_name, free_call_pid);
+		mdbg("2nd free (double free): now try to release by %s (%d) at addr 0x%08x\n", task_name, free_call_pid, free_call_addr);
+		mm_dump_node((struct mm_allocnode_s *)node, "QUARANTINED NODE");
+		mm_givesemaphore(heap);
+		return;
+	}
+#endif
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_HEAPINFO
 	heapinfo_subtract_size(heap, ((struct mm_allocnode_s *)node)->pid, ((struct mm_allocnode_s *)node)->size);
 	heapinfo_update_total_size(heap, ((-1) * ((struct mm_allocnode_s *)node)->size), ((struct mm_allocnode_s *)node)->pid);
 #endif
-	node->preceding &= ~MM_ALLOC_BIT;
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_FREEINFO
 	/* Record free metadata and quarantine sequence */
 	node->free_call_addr = free_call_addr;
 	node->free_call_pid = free_call_pid;
 #endif
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	/* Offer the chunk to the quarantine before it becomes reusable. If it is
+	 * taken, MM_ALLOC_BIT deliberately stays set: that keeps the neighbours
+	 * from merging into it and keeps malloc from finding it, so the address
+	 * cannot be handed to anybody else while it is held.
+	 */
+
+	if (mm_quarantine_add(heap, (FAR struct mm_allocnode_s *)node)) {
+		mm_givesemaphore(heap);
+		return;
+	}
+#endif
+
+	node->preceding &= ~MM_ALLOC_BIT;
+
+	mm_free_coalesce(heap, node);
+	mm_givesemaphore(heap);
+}
+
+/****************************************************************************
+ * Name: mm_free_coalesce
+ *
+ * Description:
+ *   Merge 'node' with any free neighbour and put the result on the free list.
+ *
+ *   NOTES:
+ *     (1) MM_ALLOC_BIT must already be cleared in node->preceding.
+ *     (2) the caller must hold the MM semaphore, and still holds it on
+ *         return.
+ *
+ ****************************************************************************/
+
+void mm_free_coalesce(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR struct mm_freenode_s *node)
+{
+	FAR struct mm_freenode_s *prev;
+	FAR struct mm_freenode_s *next;
+
 	/* Check if the following node is free and, if so, merge it */
 
 	next = (FAR struct mm_freenode_s *)((char *)node + node->size);
@@ -252,7 +306,6 @@ static void mm_free_internal(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR void *mem, mmaddres
 	/* Add the merged node to the nodelist */
 
 	mm_addfreechunk(heap, node);
-	mm_givesemaphore(heap);
 }
 
 /****************************************************************************
diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_initialize.c b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_initialize.c
index 692de114..250f13b1 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_initialize.c
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_initialize.c
@@ -254,6 +254,10 @@ int mm_initialize(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, FAR void *heapstart, size_t heapsi
 		heap->mm_delaylist[i] = NULL;
 	}
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	mm_quarantine_init(heap);
+#endif
+
 	/* Initialize the malloc semaphore to one (to support one-at-
 	 * a-time access to private data sets).
 	 */
diff --git a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_malloc.c b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_malloc.c
index 0420cc87..1d259a2f 100644
--- a/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_malloc.c
+++ b/os/mm/mm_heap/mm_malloc.c
@@ -149,6 +149,9 @@ FAR void *mm_malloc(FAR struct mm_heap_s *heap, size_t size, mmaddress_t caller_
 	void *ret = NULL;
 	int ndx;
 	bool gc_done = false;
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	bool qflush_done = false;
+#endif
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_HEAPINFO
 	size_t gc_before_size;
 #endif
@@ -297,6 +300,29 @@ retry_after_gc:
 
 	mm_givesemaphore(heap);
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_QUARANTINE
+	if (!ret && qflush_done == false) {
+		/* Before anything more drastic, hand back the memory which is only
+		 * being held for use-after-free detection. Without this step the
+		 * quarantine would turn into allocation failures which do not happen
+		 * with the option disabled.
+		 */
+
+		size_t qfreed;
+
+		mm_takesemaphore(heap);
+		qfreed = mm_quarantine_flush(heap);
+		mm_givesemaphore(heap);
+
+		qflush_done = true;
+
+		if (qfreed > 0) {
+			mdbg("Allocation failed, released %u bytes from the quarantine\n", qfreed);
+			goto retry_after_gc;
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 	if (!ret && gc_done == false) {
 		mdbg("Allocation failed!!! We dont have enough memory. Try to free dead task stack areas\n");
 #ifdef CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_HEAPINFO
